*======================================================================
* modules.a99 - the module system: MODCALL (call into another module and
* come back), GOMOD (switch to another module).
*
* A module is 8K of code in two SAMS pages, mapped into slot C
* (>C000-DFFF).  Its first words are the entry table:
*   >C000 INIT     called when the module is switched to (R0 = the
*                  previous module's first page, 0 at boot; R1 = the
*                  argument GOMOD gave, 0 at boot)
*   >C002 TICK     called once a frame; R0 = the next key code (high
*                  byte) or 0
*   >C004 REDRAW   by convention: draw the whole screen again (the kernel
*                  never calls it; a module calls it after something
*                  covered its screen)
*   >C006 reserved
*   >C008 ...      the module's own entries, for MODCALL
* Only the kernel's main loop remaps slot C, so a module never maps
* itself: it asks (GOMOD) or it borrows another module for one call
* (MODCALL).
*======================================================================

* TWO WAYS TO USE ANOTHER MODULE.  GOMOD means 'become that module': the
* screen and the keys are handed over and the old module stops running (until
* someone switches back).  MODCALL means 'borrow that module for one
* subroutine call': the other module's code is mapped at >C000 for the length
* of the call and the caller is mapped back afterwards, so the caller keeps
* running.  The workbench shell uses MODCALL to run the assembler (a
* subroutine that returns a result) and GOMOD to hand the screen to the
* editor.
*
* How MODCALL works, step by step: remember which pages are in slot C now (on
* the stack), map the other module's pages there (PAGEC), fetch the routine's
* address from the module's entry table and call it with BL through a
* register, keep what it returned in R0, map the caller back and return.  The
* caller's own code is unmapped for the duration of the call, which is why a
* called routine must never assume the caller's code or labels are there, and
* why arguments go in registers or APPRAM and never as pointers into the
* caller's code.  The pages are given as page NUMBERS: the build assigns them
* and writes PG_<NAME> equates for each module into kernel.equ.
* MODCALL - call entry R1 (the ADDRESS of a word in a module's entry
* table: >C000 + 2 * the entry's number) of the module in pages R0/R0+1,
* then map the calling module back.  R0 returns from the call; the other
* registers are whatever the called routine left, so pass arguments in
* R2-R9 or in memory.  Calls nest.  A module may not call another's
* routines directly (they are not mapped): use this.  The .callmod macro
* (macros.a99) loads R0 and R1 for you.  Uses everything the called
* routine does.
MODCALL .enter
       MOV  @CURCC,R2          the calling module's first page ...
       .push R2                ... and what window >D000 holds, kept: each
       MOV  @CURCD,R2          window is put back by itself afterwards, so
       .push R2                a callee of either size leaves both as they were
       MOV  R1,@MCENT          (the entry's address)
       BL   @PAGEC             map the module
       MOV  @MCENT,R1
       MOV  *R1,R1             its entry: the routine's address
       BL   *R1                call it
       MOV  R0,@MCRET          its result, kept
       .pop R5                 the caller's >D000
       .pop R0                 and its >C000, back
       BL   @MAPC
       MOV  R5,R0
       BL   @MAPD
       MOV  @MCRET,R0          the result
       .leave
       RT

* WHY GOMOD DOES NOT SWITCH AT ONCE.  A routine that calls GOMOD is itself
* running from slot C: if the mapping changed under it, the next instruction
* it fetched would be from the new module.  So GOMOD only records the request
* (NEXTPG, GOARG); the kernel's main loop performs the switch after the
* module's TICK has returned, between frames, when nothing of the old module
* is running.  The consequence to remember: code after a GOMOD call still
* runs, and in the same module, until it returns.
* GOMOD - switch to the module whose first page is R0, handing it R1 as
* its argument.  Takes effect when the calling TICK returns to the main
* loop (so the caller finishes its work first, and may still be running
* in slot C when it calls this).  The target's INIT is called with R0 =
* the caller's first page and R1 = the argument; PREVPG holds the
* caller's page afterwards, so a module can go "back" with GOMOD PREVPG.
* Leaf.  Uses nothing.
GOMOD  MOV  R0,@NEXTPG
       MOV  R1,@GOARG
       RT
